  "client_view_text": " Here\u2019s a write-ahead log example:\nSuppose a database has a single table for storing customer records, called \u201ccustomer\u201d. A write-ahead log, or WAL, is a database feature that ensures that all changes are recorded to a log before they are made permanent to the main database. This allows the database to recover from a crash by replaying the transactions from the log. Here's how it works:\n\n## Step 1: Description of the Write-Ahead Log\nA write-ahead log (WAL) is a database feature that records all changes to the database in a log before making them permanent to the main database. This log is essentially a journal of all transactions made to the database, including insertions, deletions, and modifications.\n\n## Step 2: Transaction Process with WAL\nWhen a database transaction is initiated, the database first records the transaction in the write-ahead log. Only after the transaction is successfully recorded in the log does the database make the changes permanent to the main database. This ensures that the database always has a complete and consistent log of all transactions, regardless of the outcome.\n\n## Step 3: Recovery Process After a Crash\nIn the event of a crash, the database can recover by replaying the transactions recorded in the WAL. This process involves reading the WAL from the point where it was last updated before the crash and applying each transaction to the database as if it were"
